# Plattegrond Studio — copy to .env.local and fill in what you need.
#
# With neither Supabase variable set the app runs in local mode: no accounts, no
# gate, plans in localStorage, renders in IndexedDB. That is not a degraded
# fallback — it is what CI, the Playwright suite and anyone running the editor on
# their own machine get, and it stays supported.
#
# `next dev` and `next build` read .env.local, which .gitignore covers via
# `.env*.local`. Copy this file to .env.local and fill it in.
#
# Full walkthrough: docs/SUPABASE.md

# ── Supabase — set both of these, or neither ──────────────────────────────────

# Project URL, from Project Settings → API (newer dashboards: Data API).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# The publishable key from the same page — the one that is safe in a browser,
# because row-level security is what protects the data, not this string. Older
# dashboards call it the anon key, and NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY is still
# read as a fallback so an existing .env keeps working.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# There is deliberately no service_role key here. Nothing in this app reads one,
# and a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix on it would hand every visitor the whole database.

# ── The render provider ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# From dashboard.bfl.ai/api/keys. Server-only — never prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_, and
# never returned to the browser. Without it /api/render refuses every render and
# says so; everything else in the app works.
FLUX_API_KEY=

# ── Running without accounts on purpose ───────────────────────────────────────

# A production build with no Supabase credentials is refused with HTTP 503
# instead of quietly serving one shared editor to everyone. Set this to `local`
# to say you meant it — a deliberate single-user deployment. Anything else, or
# empty, leaves the refusal in place. Not needed for `pnpm dev`, which is local
# mode already.
PLATTEGROND_MODE=

# ── Gone ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# APP_LOGIN and SESSION_SECRET were the single shared password and the cookie it
# signed. Accounts replaced both; nothing reads them any more. Delete them from
# any .env you already have.

# ── mail ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Not read by the app. Supabase sends the sign-in codes, so the SMTP
# credentials live in the Supabase project's own auth config, not here —
# see docs/SUPABASE.md step 5. This is kept only so whoever holds the
# provider key can re-run that configuration.
RESEND_API_KEY=
